**Persistence/pairing attack.** Every two-by-two graph has exactly one bispecial image in each of the four zero/positive regimes, and its multiplicity is preserved: structural.py checks all 252 graph/regime cases. This also has a short set proof: among the left candidate maps exactly one is injective on any fixed two-element left set; the analogous statement holds on the right. The chosen maps depend only on the vertex sets. Thus a strong K2,2 and a weak two-edge matching with the SAME left/right vertex sets receive identical affixes at all subsequent desubstitution levels. If the weak word has Parikh vector equal to the strong word plus a nonnegative vector, their length order persists under every outer substitution. In the six-edge example, AC and AD have the same vertex sets and Parikh difference k e2. They give a persistent ordered strong/weak pair; BC is an additional strong lineage.

This reduces a plausible universal route to controlling the overlap of births of these ordered pairs, together with the zero-age weak seeds and the surviving strong lineages. A sufficient invariant would keep the total active positive excess at most one above the baseline two. We did NOT prove that invariant, nor enumerate every admissible zero-age signature. Three-free words lie inside blocks 1^(j+1),2^(k+1)1^j,2^k1^j, so their right-extension set has at most two letters unless the word is empty. That observation localizes three-right-extension branching to descendants of empty seeds, but does not order births across different depths. Empty words and j=k=0 stretches are essential.

**Right-special-chain attack.** Section9 succeeds because each right-special type has one nested suffix chain. Here a three-right-extension ancestor splits into two types, and source Proposition28 permits four bispecial descendants. We found no bound on the number of simultaneously active suffix chains. Merely proposing three chains restates a sufficient stronger slope bound. A violation of p(n+1)-p(n)<=3 alone would NOT refute p(n)<=3n.

**Checks and limits.** 110 fixed-seed finite directive prefixes through n=46 yielded no bound violation. Maximum sampled first difference was3. Sixteen stationary double-block languages were computed exactly through length14 and satisfy the bound there. For stationary sigma, closure of factors of sigma(F) through N is exact: any N-factor in sigma(w) is covered by the image of a factor of w of length at most N, since sigma is nonerasing. A fixed point containing all letters therefore contains all finite iterates, and every generated factor comes from an iterate. Binary prefix products inside one period are contained at a later full period by alphabet coverage. This certifies these finite N-factor languages only; it is not a universal/limit proof. No computational claim beyond the stored outputs.

**Strongest classical adaptation objection.** The source already suggests signed bispecial enumeration, and its preceding Sections6 and9 provide cancellation and suffix-chain templates. Graph conservation, a unique two-by-two descendant, positive Parikh order and finite-prefix monotonicity are ordinary adaptations of those techniques. None earns independent credit. To nominate an outcome, one must supply the missing global length-order invariant (including non-Gauss tails) or an exact finite prefix with p(n)>3n, and then conduct a current complete prior-art review. No such endpoint is claimed.
